    "answer": {
      "state": "completed",
      "text": "Decision for the disposition log: downgrade this incident from the CRITICAL alert level to the lowest severity tier, recorded as benign / non-security (false positive). Liam Kowalski, as On-Call Incident Commander, should log it this way.\n\nThe released evidence does not include the organization's tier taxonomy, so the exact tier label should come from the standard scale.\n\nWhy:\n1. The 02:47 UTC PagerDuty CRITICAL alert for Meridian Retail Tier-1 Surfaces was an automated threshold breach on ingress metrics. It flagged an anomaly but did not show compromise.\n2. Unauthorized access to customer data has not been confirmed, and the traces follow a benign path.\n3. The threat intelligence assessment found no active exploitation indicators and matched no TTPs (EXPLOIT_ABSENT_NO_TTPS_MATCHED).\n4. Omar Okonkwo's record marks the activity as confirmed benign, because the deploy ledger matches both its timing and its source.\n\nRelated context for the log: a contracted red team is actively testing Meridian Retail's customer-facing services as part of the annual penetration test, and its engagement on the API gateway ends tomorrow morning. The log should record this overlap. If any ingress anomaly appears that the deploy ledger does not explain, the incident should be re-triaged, with the red team engagement as a possible source, before the engagement closes.",
